  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nA dreary day across most of southern and eastern Nebraska and\nnorth central Kansas behind the cold front that passed through\nthis morning. As of 3pm the cold front has passed through the\nwhole forecast area, with winds turning north at Beloit, KS.\nAlong and behind the cold front there is plentiful stratus and\naccompanying drizzle/light rain. Winds are generally 10-15 mph \nbehind the front. Temps are in the 40s across the area. \n\nAlong and south of the front in southern and central Kansas, a\nfew scattered showers are developing, and this activity is\nexpected to continue to develop northeastward along the front\ninto southeastern Nebraska. This activity could clip the\nfarthest southeast counties of the forecast area (Mitchell\nCounty/Thayer County) bringing the potential for higher \nrainfall totals (0.25\"), elsewhere, rainfall totals less than a\ntenth of an inch are expected. This storm system will continue \nto progress eastward, taking the precip and cloud cover with it,\nbut leaving behind the colder air. \n\nAfter a generally mild October and early November, temperatures\nthroughout the next week will be in the upper 30s to low 40s\nthrough about Thanksgiving before inching warmer for next\nweekend closer to 50 degrees as high temperatures. Low\ntemperatures in the 20s are expected throughout the period. \n\nDespite the generally zonal flow that develops behind this\nsystem today, precipitation chances are hard to come by. There\nare a number of waves moving across the plains, but with little\nmoisture to work with, not much precip is expected. \n\nThe next major system will move towards the area Monday and\nacross the Plains on Tuesday, there is a chance for some light\nprecip, but this is not likely, 10-15%. After Tuesday's system a\npotent disturbance dives quickly southward from the northern\nPlains on Wednesday night into early Thanksgiving day. This\npotent system has some slightly cooler air and a weak backdoor\nfront may move across the area. Again, similar to the system on\nTuesday, there is little in the way of moisture, so the\natmosphere isn't able to squeeze much out. That being said,\nnortheast NE could see some light precip Wednesday night. This \nstays out of our forecast area.",
